// Destination summaries for the card, one per stop (data contract §18, summary: gap).
// Source: the direct answer (40 to 60 words) for each place in sydney-towns-aeo.xlsx, from its page on sydney.com or
// visitnsw.com, read on 22 November 2026. Generated by ../tools/gen-destinations.py; edit the sheet, not here. A stop with no entry
// shows no summary (the empty state). Distances and images are still placeholders.
const SUMMARIES = {
  "The Rocks": "The Rocks is Sydney's oldest neighbourhood and the birthplace of modern Sydney, settled in 1788. Beside Circular Quay, it pairs cobbled laneways, sandstone buildings and the city's oldest pubs with the Museum of Contemporary Art, weekend markets and close-up views of the Sydney Harbour Bridge and Opera House.",
  "Darling Harbour": "Darling Harbour is a family-friendly waterside precinct a 10-minute walk from Town Hall Station. It has four kids' attractions within walking distance: SEA LIFE Sydney Aquarium, WILD LIFE Sydney Zoo, Madame Tussauds and Darling Quarter Kids Playground. There is also the Australian National Maritime Museum, the Chinese Garden of Friendship and ICC Sydney.",
  "Surry Hills": "Surry Hills is an inner Sydney suburb a 10-minute walk from Central Station, known for its restaurants, small bars and coffee culture. Crown Street has Good Food Guide-listed venues, alongside vintage boutiques, street art, the Brett Whiteley Studio and Belvoir St Theatre.",
  "Newtown": "Newtown is a bohemian inner west Sydney suburb about 10 minutes by train from the city. King Street is its heart, packed with vintage shops, bookstores, multicultural restaurants and bars. It is also home to Enmore Theatre, Sydney's oldest running theatre, and plenty of street art.",
  "Bondi": "Bondi is Sydney's world-famous beach suburb, about 30 minutes by public transport from Town Hall. It is home to the Bondi Icebergs ocean pool, the world's oldest surf lifesaving club (1907), year-round surf and the start of the 6km Bondi to Coogee Coastal Walk.",
  "Coogee": "Coogee is a relaxed beach suburb in Sydney's east, a little more chilled than Bondi. It has a white sand beach, historic ocean pools including McIver Baths and Wylie's Baths, snorkelling at Gordons Bay, and is the start or finish of the Bondi to Coogee Coastal Walk.",
  "Sydney Olympic Park": "Sydney Olympic Park is the sporting and entertainment precinct built for the 2000 Olympic Games, about 30 minutes by train from Central Station. Visit Accor Stadium, swim in the pool where Ian Thorpe won gold, surf at URBNSURF, cycle 35km of paths in Bicentennial Park, and catch the Sydney Royal Easter Show.",
  "Manly": "Manly is a beachside suburb on Sydney's Northern Beaches, 30 minutes by ferry from Circular Quay. Its 3km surf beach hosted the world's first surfing contest in 1964. The Corso links the wharf to the beach, and Shelly Beach offers top snorkelling in the Cabbage Tree Bay aquatic reserve.",
  "Parramatta": "Parramatta is Sydney's second CBD and the heart of Western Sydney, about 25 minutes by train from Central. It has UNESCO World Heritage-listed convict sites including Old Government House, the new Powerhouse Parramatta museum, Riverside Theatre, Elizabeth Farm and multicultural eat streets including Harris Park's 'Little India'.",
  "Cronulla": "Cronulla is a peninsula beach suburb in Sydney's south and the only Sydney beach you can reach directly by train, about 50 minutes from Central. It offers patrolled surf beaches, ocean rock pools, the 4km Cronulla Beach Walk and ferries to Bundeena and the Royal National Park.",
  "Palm Beach": "Palm Beach, or 'Palmy', is at the northern tip of Sydney's Northern Beaches and is the filming location of Home and Away. Surrounded by Pittwater, the Pacific Ocean and Broken Bay, it offers surfing, an ocean pool and the walk to Barrenjoey Lighthouse. It is 90 minutes by public transport or 20 minutes by seaplane.",
  "Terrigal": "Terrigal is a Central Coast beach town 90 minutes' drive from Sydney, known for its golden surf beach, lagoon and Norfolk Pine-lined esplanade of cafes and rooftop bars. Walk to The Skillion lookout for views and whale spotting from May to November, or learn to surf on Terrigal Beach.",
  "Lake Macquarie": "Lake Macquarie is a relaxed lakeside region about a 90-minute drive from Sydney, centred on the largest saltwater lake in the Southern Hemisphere. It suits a day trip or longer, with rainforest hikes, waterfall camping and ancient sea caves, and towns including Belmont, Charlestown, Morisset, Swansea, Warners Bay and Toronto.",
  "Newcastle": "Newcastle is a coastal city 2.5 hours' drive or just under three hours by train north of Sydney. It blends beaches, ocean baths and surf breaks with bars, street art and heritage. Walk the 6km Bathers Way from Nobbys Beach to Merewether Ocean Baths, eat at Honeysuckle and spot whales from May to November.",
  "Port Stephens": "Port Stephens is a coastal region an easy 2.5-hour drive north of Sydney, known for giant sand dunes, dolphins and whales, and 26 beaches. Towns include Nelson Bay, Shoal Bay and Anna Bay, with the Worimi Conservation Lands nearby. Newcastle Airport is 30 minutes from Nelson Bay, and a car is recommended.",
  "Forster": "Forster sits on a land spit between Wallis Lake and the Pacific Ocean, just over 3.5 hours' drive north of Sydney. It offers 40km of beaches south to Booti Booti National Park, diving with grey nurse sharks, whale watching from May to November, fishing, and Sydney Rock Oysters grown in Wallis Lake.",
  "Port Macquarie": "Port Macquarie is a mid-North Coast town four hours' drive north of Sydney, at the mouth of the Hastings River. It has the highest koala population on Australia's east coast and the famous Koala Hospital, 17 nearby beaches, whale watching from May to November and a penal-settlement history dating to 1821.",
  "Coffs Harbour": "Coffs Harbour is a family-friendly North Coast city under six hours' drive from Sydney, home to the Big Banana, Australia's first 'Big Thing' (1964). It has more than 30 beaches, diving in the Solitary Islands Marine Park, whale swims in August and September, and is the gateway to the Waterfall Way and Dorrigo rainforest.",
  "Yamba": "Yamba is a coastal town at the mouth of the Clarence River on the NSW North Coast, about 7.5 hours' drive from Sydney. It is known for Angourie Point surf, famous Yamba prawns, fishing, dolphin kayak tours and the 65km Yuraygir coastal walk, with a strong dining scene for its size.",
  "Byron Bay": "Byron Bay is a laid-back town on the far North Coast of NSW, known for its beaches, surf breaks, wellness culture and food scene. It has Cape Byron Lighthouse and Australia's easternmost beach, diving at Julian Rocks and peak whale watching from July to September. Ballina Byron Gateway Airport is 30 minutes away.",
  "Royal National Park": "Royal National Park, established in 1879, is one of the world's oldest national parks, about an hour's drive south of the Sydney CBD near Cronulla. It spans over 15,000 hectares of beaches, rainforest and Aboriginal sites. Highlights are the 26km Coast Track, Wedding Cake Rock, Wattamolla Beach and the Figure Eight Pools. A per-car entry fee applies.",
  "Stanwell Park": "Stanwell Park is a beach village on the southern edge of the Royal National Park, just over an hour from Sydney by car or train. It is internationally famous for hang gliding and paragliding from Bald Hill, where Lawrence Hargrave first flew his box kite in 1894, and is the start of the Grand Pacific Walk.",
  "Wollongong": "Wollongong is NSW's third-largest city, about 90 minutes from Sydney by car or train, set between the Illawarra Escarpment and golden beaches. It has 17 patrolled beaches, rock pools, skydiving, the Sea Cliff Bridge, Nan Tien Temple, the largest Buddhist temple in the Southern Hemisphere, and a thriving arts and food scene.",
  "Kiama": "Kiama is a South Coast seaside town about one hour 45 minutes' drive from Sydney via the Grand Pacific Drive, or just over two hours by train. Its Kiama Blowhole, thought to be the largest in the world, can blast water up to 30 metres high. It also has the 20km Kiama Coast Walk, ocean pools and weekly markets.",
  "Berry": "Berry is a stylish village in the Shoalhaven, just over two hours' drive or about three hours by train from Sydney. It is known for its charming main street, heritage buildings, the famous Berry Donut Van, the hatted South on Albany restaurant and nearby wineries like Silos Estate and Two Figs.",
  "Jervis Bay": "Jervis Bay is a South Coast bay in the Shoalhaven, less than three hours' drive from both Sydney and Canberra. Known for white sand beaches and clear water, it has two national parks and a marine park with bottlenose dolphins, fur seals, little penguins and sea dragons, plus villages including Huskisson, Vincentia and Hyams Beach.",
  "Ulladulla": "Ulladulla is a Shoalhaven harbour town three hours' drive from Sydney, a fishing port since 1859. It is known for fresh seafood, the Gondwana Coast Fossil Walk with 270-million-year-old fossils, the Coomee Nulunga Cultural Trail, the Pigeon House Mountain Didthul walk and Cupitt's Estate winery and brewery.",
  "Batemans Bay": "Batemans Bay is a South Coast town where the Clyde River meets the sea, about four hours' drive from Sydney and two from Canberra. It is at the heart of Australia's Oyster Coast, with Clyde River oysters, kayaking, a snorkelling trail, surf beaches and the new 15km Munjip Trail coastal walk.",
  "Narooma": "Narooma is a South Coast town on the turquoise Wagonga Inlet, about five hours' drive from Sydney. It is the heart of the Oyster Coast and the gateway to Montague Island (Barunguba), where you can snorkel with seals and see little penguins. Revamped venues have made it a growing foodie destination.",
  "Eden": "Eden is a Sapphire Coast town on Twofold Bay, a little over six hours' drive from Sydney, and one of Australia's best whale-watching spots from May to early November. Learn about orca Old Tom at the Eden Killer Whale Museum, and walk the revamped 32km Light to Light Walk in Beowa National Park.",
  "Penrith": "Penrith is a Western Sydney suburb on the Nepean River at the foot of the Blue Mountains, about an hour from Sydney by train. It is known for adventure, with whitewater rafting at Penrith Whitewater Stadium, indoor skydiving at iFLY and wakeboarding, plus the Penrith Regional Gallery and Joan Sutherland Performing Arts Centre.",
  "Glenbrook": "Glenbrook is the eastern gateway to the Blue Mountains and the entry point to the Glenbrook area of the national park. It is ideal for bushwalking and mountain biking, with camping at Euroka Clearing, walks to Glenbrook Creek waterholes and Red Hands Cave, lookouts like Tunnel View, and a charming village of shops and cafes.",
  "Katoomba": "Katoomba is the main town of the World Heritage-listed Blue Mountains, a 90-minute drive or two-hour train ride from Sydney. It is the base for Echo Point and the Three Sisters, Scenic World's steep railway and Skyway, and bushwalks into the Jamison Valley, with Art Deco streets, bars and breweries.",
  "Lithgow": "Lithgow is an industrial heritage town just west of the Blue Mountains, about 2.5 hours' drive or three hours by train from Sydney. It is the gateway to Wollemi and Gardens of Stone national parks, the vast Capertee Valley and Jenolan Caves, with the Zig Zag Railway, Hassans Walls lookout and the Small Arms Factory Museum.",
  "Bathurst": "Bathurst is Australia's oldest inland colonial settlement, just over three hours' drive west of Sydney. It is the home of Australian motor racing at Mount Panorama-Wahluu, host of the Bathurst 1000, and is rich in gold rush heritage, with the Australian Fossil and Mineral Museum, Abercrombie House and nearby historic gold towns.",
  "Orange": "Orange is a Central West country town about 3.5 hours' drive from Sydney, famed for cool-climate wines, rich produce and autumn colour. It has 80 vineyards and 30 cellar doors, acclaimed restaurants, the Orange Botanic Gardens and walks on Mount Canobolas, an ancient volcano. Key events are FOOD Week in April and the Wine Festival in October.",
  "Parkes": "Parkes is a Central West heritage town just under five hours' drive from Sydney, famous for the CSIRO Parkes radio telescope, 'The Dish', which helped broadcast the 1969 Moon landing. Every January the Parkes Elvis Festival draws impersonators and fans for Elvis's birthday. The King's Castle exhibit holds his personal artefacts.",
  "Dubbo": "Dubbo is a regional city on the Macquarie River, about five hours' drive northwest of Sydney. It is home to Taronga Western Plains Zoo, one of Australia's largest zoos with more than 5,000 animals and overnight safari stays, plus the heritage-listed Old Dubbo Gaol, the Royal Flying Doctor Service Visitor Experience and stargazing.",
  "Cobar": "Cobar, known as 'The Copper City', is an outback town in central NSW about eight hours' drive from Sydney. Once Australia's largest copper producer, it has the Great Cobar Museum, the Fort Bourke Hill open-cut mine lookout, heritage buildings and the Cobar Sound Chapel. It is also the gateway to Mount Grenfell's Aboriginal rock art.",
  "Broken Hill": "Broken Hill is a famous outback city and Australia's first heritage-listed city, about 13 hours' drive or a flight from Sydney. Australia's oldest mining town, it has the Line of Lode Miners Memorial, the oldest regional gallery in NSW, the Living Desert Sculptures and the Palace Hotel from Priscilla, Queen of the Desert.",
  "Honeysuckle": "Honeysuckle is Newcastle's waterfront entertainment precinct, with cafes, restaurants, hotels, boardwalks and a marina on the harbour. Its foreshore promenades suit walking, cycling or rollerblading while you watch the busy harbour, and it is home to Newcastle Museum, one of the city's most popular attractions.",
  "Nobbys Beach": "Nobbys Beach is Newcastle's highest-profile beach, popular with families, learner surfers and local dolphins. Walk the break-wall beneath the iconic Nobbys Lighthouse and you may see tugs guide coal ships into the harbour. It has barbecues and a kiosk, marks the northern end of the Bathers Way, and is reached by Light Rail or car.",
  "Bar Beach": "Bar Beach is a beachside suburb in southwest Newcastle, named after a rock pool formation called The Bar. It has a strong surf and sporting heritage, including the heritage-listed Cooks Hill Surf Life Saving Club, and its northern end is named after the Susan Gilmore, an American ship wrecked there in 1884.",
  "Hamilton": "Hamilton is a Newcastle suburb known as a cosmopolitan dining precinct. Originally home to Newcastle's first Italian and Greek immigrants, its restaurants, cafes and delis reflect that Mediterranean heritage. Beaumont Street is the hub, with sidewalk cafes, pubs, boutiques, antiques, bookshops and homewares.",
  "Merewether": "Merewether Beach in Newcastle is home to Surfest, an international surfing competition, and to Merewether Ocean Baths, the largest ocean baths complex in the Southern Hemisphere. It sits in a beach reserve from Dixon Park to Burwood Beach and marks the southern end of the Bathers Way coastal walk.",
  "Charlestown": "Charlestown is the largest town in the Lake Macquarie region, just over two hours' drive from Sydney and 20 minutes from Newcastle. Home to Charlestown Square, the Hunter's largest shopping centre, it is also a base for Glenrock State Conservation Area's rainforest walks and mountain bike trails, and for lakeside cycling.",
  "Warners Bay": "Warners Bay is a lakeside suburb on Lake Macquarie, just under two hours' drive from Sydney and 30 minutes from Newcastle. Its 9km waterfront path and Red Bluff Boardwalk suit walking and cycling, the Creative Lake Sculpture Trail features Australian artists, and Speers Point Park has a giant spiral slide.",
  "Redhead": "Redhead Beach, in Lake Macquarie, sits at the northern end of a nine-mile stretch of coastline and is known for its cliffs, rocky outcrops and shark tower. It is patrolled in warmer months, popular with swimmers and surfers, and dog-friendly, with a pump track, Webs Park playground and accessible Owens Walkway nearby.",
  "Raymond Terrace": "Raymond Terrace is a riverside town on the Hunter and Williams rivers in Port Stephens. King Street's grand 19th-century timber buildings show its history, and the Junction Inn was once King's Hotel, said to be the second-oldest hotel in NSW. It suits water skiing, boating and picnics, with Karuah's oysters 20 minutes north.",
  "Maitland": "Maitland is a historic Hunter River town halfway between Newcastle and the Hunter Valley vineyards, two hours' drive from Sydney. It is known for street art, Maitland Regional Art Gallery, heritage walks, the Big UGG Boots and Australia's first accredited Slow Food market, Earth Markets, plus Steamfest each April.",
  "Anna Bay": "Anna Bay is a relaxed surf village in Port Stephens, about 2.5 hours' drive north of Sydney. It is the gateway to the Stockton Bight Sand Dunes in the Worimi Conservation Lands, the largest moving coastal dunes in the Southern Hemisphere, with sandboarding, camel rides and 4WD tours, plus Birubi and One Mile surf beaches.",
  "Nelson Bay": "Nelson Bay is the largest town in Port Stephens, about 2 hours 45 minutes' drive north of Sydney. More than 100 dolphins live in its marine park, with dolphin cruises year-round, wild dolphin swims in warmer months and whale watching from May to November. Sheltered beaches suit young families.",
  "Hawks Nest": "Hawks Nest is a classic seaside town about three hours' drive north of Sydney, separated from Port Stephens by Yacaaba Headland. It has the patrolled Bennetts Beach, sheltered Jimmys Beach for families, kayaking on the Myall River and walks in Myall Lakes National Park, including Dark Point Aboriginal Place.",
  "Seal Rocks": "Seal Rocks is an isolated Barrington Coast village famous for Sugarloaf Point Lighthouse, built in 1874–75 and one of only two Australian lighthouse towers with an external stairway. It draws visitors year-round for unspoilt surf beaches, snorkelling and diving, Myall Lakes National Park and whale watching from May to October.",
  "Taree": "Taree is the main town of the Manning Valley on the Manning River, about three hours 45 minutes' drive north of Sydney. Dating to 1854, it has a heritage walk, the Manning Regional Art Gallery and Entertainment Centre, and is a base for the Forster coast and Ellenborough Falls, the tallest single-drop waterfall in NSW.",
  "South West Rocks": "South West Rocks is a quiet coastal town at the mouth of the Macleay River, about five hours' drive from Sydney. Just offshore, Fish Rock Cave is one of the world's best cave dives and a breeding ground for grey nurse sharks. Visit Trial Bay Gaol (1886), Smoky Cape Lighthouse and uncrowded beaches like Horseshoe Bay.",
  "Swansea": "Swansea sits between Lake Macquarie and the Pacific Ocean, about one hour 45 minutes' drive north of Sydney and 40 minutes from Newcastle. It is close to the sea caves of Caves Beach, Wallarah National Park's coastal walk, Blacksmiths Beach and the heritage village of Catherine Hill Bay.",
  "Catherine Hill Bay": "Catherine Hill Bay is a State Heritage-listed coal mining village just south of Swansea in Lake Macquarie. Its patrolled beach has twice been named one of 'Australia's 101 Best Beaches' and features an iconic former coal-loading jetty. Its 19th-century miners' cottages are the oldest continuous settlement in the City of Lake Macquarie.",
  "Norah Head": "Norah Head is a laid-back Central Coast town about 1.5 hours' drive from Sydney, known for its heritage-listed lighthouse, one of the last classical-style lighthouses in NSW, operating since 1903. It is a top whale-watching spot from May to November, near Wyrrabalong National Park's littoral rainforest, Soldiers Beach and rock fishing.",
  "The Entrance": "The Entrance is a family-favourite Central Coast town between the Pacific Ocean and Tuggerah Lake, about 1.5 hours' drive from Sydney. It has two heritage-listed ocean baths, the historic carousel and Vera's Water Garden, Long Jetty, the 12km Tuggerah Lakes Cycleway, and festivals including The Lakes Festival in November.",
  "Gosford": "Gosford is a regional centre on Brisbane Water on the Central Coast, just over an hour's drive or about 80 minutes by direct train from Sydney. It is home to the Australian Reptile Park, the Gosford Regional Gallery and Japanese garden, craft breweries, and walks in Brisbane Water National Park including Somersby Falls.",
  "Brooklyn": "Brooklyn is a riverside village on the Hawkesbury River in Sydney's north, beside Hawkesbury River train station. It is the base for river cruises, kayaking, boat hire for fishing, oyster farm tours and the ferry to car-free Dangar Island.",
  "Sydney": "Sydney is the NSW capital and one of the world's most multicultural cities, fanning out from Sydney Harbour to Bondi in the east, Palm Beach in the north, Western Sydney adventures and Royal National Park in the south. Start in Sydney City at the Opera House and Circular Quay, where ferries reach Taronga Zoo, Manly and Watsons Bay.",
  "Cessnock": "Cessnock is the largest town in the Hunter Valley, about two hours' drive north of Sydney. There are no wineries in town itself, but it is the base for many wine tours and a short drive to cellar doors. It is also home to the Hunter Valley Wildlife Park, Bistro Molines and the Valley Brewhouse.",
  "Pokolbin": "Pokolbin is the wine capital of the Hunter Valley, Australia's oldest wine region, about two hours' drive from Sydney. It has the region's highest concentration of wineries, including Tyrrell's, McGuigan and Audrey Wilkinson, plus the two-hat Muse Restaurant, Hunter Valley Gardens and sunrise hot air balloon flights.",
  "Wollombi": "Wollombi is a historic Hunter Valley village established in 1830, just under two hours' drive from Sydney. Once a junction on the convict-built Great North Road, it has well-preserved 19th-century streets, the Wollombi Endeavour Museum, a creative community, boutique wineries and the World Heritage-listed Yengo National Park nearby.",
  "Singleton": "Singleton is a riverside town on the northern edge of the Hunter Valley wine region, about 2.5 hours' drive from Sydney. Founded over 200 years ago, it has a heritage walk, the Australian Army Infantry Museum, the largest sundial in the Southern Hemisphere and the Singleton Arts and Cultural Centre, opened in 2022.",
  "Muswellbrook": "Muswellbrook is an Upper Hunter town three hours' drive from Sydney, set among vineyards. Taste handmade cheese at Hunter Belle, olive oils from Pukara Estate and nearby Denman wines. Walk the 4.5km Heritage Walk and see the Max Watter Collection at the Muswellbrook Regional Arts Centre. A direct train runs from Sydney.",
  "Denman": "Denman is a small Upper Hunter town just under three hours' drive from Sydney, known for boutique wineries, horse studs and heritage charm. Visit Small Forest, run by Japan's first female winemaker, Hollydene Estate near the Coolmore stud that raised Winx, and nearby Wollemi and Goulburn River national parks.",
  "Scone": "Scone is known as the horse capital of Australia, an Upper Hunter town just over three hours' drive or four hours by train from Sydney. It is one of the world's biggest horse-breeding regions, where champion mare Winx was raised. It hosts the 10-day Scone Horse Festival in May and is near Burning Mountain.",
  "Mudgee": "Mudgee is a Central West town about 3.5 hours' drive northwest of Sydney, recognised as one of Australia's great food and wine destinations. It has more than 35 wineries and cellar doors, 19th-century heritage streets, the new Mudgee Arts Precinct and nearby Wollemi National Park, plus direct flights from Sydney six days a week.",
  "Tamworth": "Tamworth is the country music capital in the New England region, five hours' drive north of Sydney. Every January it hosts the 10-day Tamworth Country Music Festival, the largest in the Southern Hemisphere, with more than 3,000 events. Year-round, visit the 12-metre Big Golden Guitar, the Hands of Fame Park and the Country Music Hall of Fame.",
  "Gunnedah": "Gunnedah is a country town on the Namoi River, about five hours' drive from Sydney and one hour from Tamworth. It inspired Dorothea Mackellar's poem 'My Country', has a thriving wild koala population, hosts AgQuip, one of Australia's biggest agricultural shows, and is near Lake Keepit for boating and gliding."
};
